<meta type=article title="External DSL handlers: <mermaid> and <abc> (Phase 2 slice 2c)">
</meta>

<section | Mermaid flowchart>

A simple flowchart authored with `<mermaid>`. The handler emits a
`<pre class="mermaid" data-acadamark-dsl="mermaid">` wrapper preserving
the source. Mermaid's CDN library (if loaded by the consumer) scans for
`class="mermaid"` and replaces the content with rendered SVG at view
time; a build-time pre-render pass finds the same blocks via
`data-acadamark-dsl="mermaid"`.

<mermaid #diagram:simple-flow>
graph LR
  A[Start] --> B{Decision}
  B -->|yes| C[OK]
  B -->|no| D[Stop]
</mermaid>

<section | Mermaid sequence diagram>

A different diagram type confirms the handler doesn't assume any
specific Mermaid grammar — it preserves whatever source the author
writes.

<mermaid>
sequenceDiagram
  Alice->>Bob: Hello Bob
  Bob-->>Alice: Hello Alice
</mermaid>

<section | ABC music notation>

A short ABC notation excerpt. The handler emits a
`<div class="abc" data-acadamark-dsl="abc">` wrapper preserving the
source. abcjs has no DOM auto-scan; the consumer's page needs a small
init script (sample in the vocab entry) that finds each marked element
and calls `ABCJS.renderAbc`.

<abc #music:twinkle>
X:1
T:Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star
M:4/4
L:1/4
K:C
C C G G | A A G2 | F F E E | D D C2 |
</abc>

This fixture exercises both external DSLs end-to-end. The handlers
preserve source verbatim and add the CDN-compatible class plus the
acadamark-specific `data-acadamark-dsl` marker. No rendering happens
at acadamark time — that's the whole point of "external."
